# netsurf: the attribute-reference scan reads escapes, skips empty sheets, finds :hover/:focus companions inert (patch 510)
# Generated 2026-11-28
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 509)
#
# An attribute change is answered without a restyle when no sheet can
# select on the attribute (patch 310's html_rl_sheet_scan_attr, a scan of
# each sheet's text). Three exact refinements:
#
# - Escapes. Any backslash outside a string made the scan answer "it might"
#   for every name, so on a utility-class sheet (.md\:flex,
#   .focus-within\:bg-x, .w-\[20px\]) every attribute was sheet-visible.
#   An escape is skipped as CSS reads it (up to six hex digits and one
#   whitespace, or the escaped character), so an escaped '[' is no
#   attribute selector either; an attribute selector's own name is read
#   with its escapes decoded and compared whole (html_rl_attr_name_at).
#   Still "it might": an escape beyond ASCII, a namespace prefix spelled
#   with an escape.
# - Empty sheets. A sheet with no source counted as unreadable; one whose
#   content is DONE is empty and references nothing (still loading: still
#   "it might").
# - Dynamic pseudo-classes. An attribute whose compound also holds, as its
#   own member, :hover, :active, :focus, :focus-visible or :focus-within
#   cannot change a box's style: box selection never matches those
#   (node_is_hover / _active / _focus answer only for the dyn_*_node a
#   hover or focus restyle sets). In :not() / :is() the same holds.
#   ":not(:focus)" is no member: that occurrence counts.
#
# old.reddit.com/r/vita (now its login page) runs, on about half its
# loads, a script loop that sets tabindex 1,094 times: each was a subtree
# restyle and full layout (one Tailwind escape at byte 104,838 of a sheet,
# plus two empty <style>s). With 510 each is "nothing to rebuild": main
# thread 15.2 s -> 9.4 s in the sim on those loads (the rest is the loop's
# own script); the quiet loads stay ~2 s.
#
# Gate: JS-attr-scan / CSS-attr-scan-ref (four steps, row for row against
# the same steps at parse time; the inert, unreferenced, live and
# escape-spelled cases each logged). The attribute gate pages (370, 409,
# 417, 433, 468) give the same counts on 509 and 510.
